#!/usr/bin/env ruby
# frozen_string_literal: true

# NO ARCHAEOLOGY ON A PUBLISHED PAGE (K-1491).
#
# This repository IS the published surface. Everything it tracks is served: the
# landing page, the narrative specification, the formal specification and its
# schemas, the problem-code vocabulary pages, the skill an assistant author
# reads, and every frozen cut of that skill. Its reader holds this repository
# and nothing else. So a sentence here that cites a row of an issue ledger, a
# numbered architecture decision, or the judgement of a person by name, is a
# pointer that resolves for nobody who will ever have the file — and a
# paragraph in a language the page is not published in is the same defect one
# layer worse, because the reader cannot even tell whether it binds them.
#
# WHY IT IS A GUARD AND NOT A CONVENTION. The sibling repository has held this
# rule since T-186. This one held it nowhere. The consequence was measured
# before this file existed: two `description` strings an assistant author reads
# out of a JSON Schema, seventeen lines of a schema validator, thirteen
# ledger-row citations, an architecture-decision reference in the formal
# specification, and two attributions of a normative choice to the maintainer by
# name — all of it drained by hand, none of it held by anything afterwards. The
# repository whose entire purpose is to be read by strangers was the one with no
# rule against citing what strangers cannot see.
#
# THIS IS NOT A TWIN. `bin/check-publication-paths` is byte-identical in both
# published repositories and is checked as such. This file is not that: it is
# the same RULE carried into a different repository, and it is deliberately
# named differently so nobody tries to reconcile the two. What differs is
# everything the rule touches — the file set, the exemptions, and which of the
# sibling's rules can be carried at all (see NOT PORTED, below).
#
# ── THE SCOPE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `git ls-files` in the repository this script lives in, because what is
# published is what git carries, and the run must happen INSIDE the repository:
# a pathspec aimed at a sibling directory from one level up matches nothing and
# exits like a clean tree.
#
# Minus, in order:
#
#   * anything that is not TEXT, decided from the BYTES rather than from a list
#     of extensions — a file carrying a NUL, or one that is not valid UTF-8. It
#     is not a formality: the three icons this site serves each contain a byte
#     run that the Cyrillic rule matches, so a byte-scan of them would report
#     three violations nobody can repair and no reader can see. The skip proves
#     itself on every run (see THE DETECTOR ARMS): a fabricated non-text blob
#     carrying a citation must be refused, and a valid Cyrillic sentence must be
#     READ, because a reader that dropped non-ASCII text would switch the
#     Cyrillic rule off while reporting a clean zero.
#
#   * the five declared EXEMPTIONS below, each with its reason. Every run
#     reports how many tracked files each one covers and FAILS on one that
#     covers none, so an exemption whose subject has left the repository says so
#     instead of sitting here as a decision about nothing.
#
# ── WHAT IS EXEMPT, DECLARED WITH ITS REASON ─────────────────────────────────
#
#   * `bin/` — maintainer tooling, and the sibling repository's standing
#     exemption for the same directory. A guard's header is where the reason a
#     rule exists is written down, and the reason is frequently the row that
#     bought it.
#   * `.github/` — the same class one step over. A workflow file is read by a
#     maintainer deciding whether a job earns its minutes, and its header cites
#     what went wrong the last time the job was absent. Nothing under it is
#     served to a reader of the site.
#   * `CHANGELOG.md` — the sanctioned afterlife. The rule that a public artefact
#     describes what IS carries exactly one exception, and this is it: the
#     removed thing gets one changelog line and nothing else.
#   * `CLAUDE.md` — a maintainer document, addressed to whoever edits this
#     repository next rather than to whoever reads it.
#   * `LICENSE` — verbatim third-party licence text. This repository may not
#     edit a word of it, so a rule reaching it could only ever produce a red
#     tree with no legal move.
#
# AND WHAT IS *NOT* EXEMPT, WHICH NEEDS ITS REASON WRITTEN DOWN JUST AS MUCH
# (K-1500). Two of the exemptions above are granted for KIND — `bin/` and
# `.github/` are maintainer tooling, read by whoever maintains this repository
# and served to nobody. That reason does not generalise, and the file it stops
# at is `spec/schemas/validate.sh`: a shell script, maintainer tooling by every
# other measure, and IN SCOPE. It is in scope because of where it lives.
# Everything under `spec/` is published beside the formal specification — a
# porter who fetches the schemas fetches the validator that proves them and may
# run it — so it is a published surface whatever else it also is, and a ledger
# citation or a paragraph of Cyrillic in it resolves for nobody who has the
# file. That is the boundary: EXEMPTION IS BY WHETHER THE FILE IS SERVED, NEVER
# BY WHETHER IT IS TOOLING. `bin/` and `.github/` qualify because nothing under
# them ships, not because of what they contain.
#
# The rule was decided the right way round before it was written down — one
# census line in that script was repaired under this guard rather than exempted
# — and a decision nobody wrote down gets re-litigated by the next person who
# meets a red line there. So it is checked as well as stated: a self-test arm
# asserts that NO exemption reaches under `spec/`, and a second asserts the
# tracked tree there still holds a file that is neither schema nor prose, so the
# boundary cannot quietly become a statement about nothing.
#
# AND ONE CARVE-OUT INSIDE A RULE, rather than over a path: the site's own
# BYLINE. A page states its author the way a package states it in a manifest
# field, and R4 is about prose that asks a reader to trust a person instead of
# reading a property. So a name that is the TEXT OF A LINK is a credit and is
# not a violation; the same name in a sentence is. The carve-out is by SHAPE,
# not by path, so it means the same on any page that grows a credit. Its
# declared limit, because an over-claimed gate is worse than none: an
# attribution deliberately wrapped in an anchor would pass, and this rule will
# never see it.
#
# ── WHAT IS ASSERTED ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   R1  no ledger id — /\b[KT]-\d{3,4}\b/. The ledger is private and always
#       will be; the id names nothing a reader can open.
#   R2  no reference to a numbered architecture decision — /\bADR-\d{4}\b/.
#       Those live in a private repository. State the rule it decided.
#   R3  no Cyrillic — /[Ѐ-ӿ]/. This project's working language is Russian and
#       its published language is English. Guillemets, em dashes, arrows and
#       accented Latin are not Cyrillic and stay, so the rule cannot argue with
#       the house punctuation.
#   R4  no maintainer named in prose — /\bPhil\b/, outside the byline shape.
#   R5  no sentence NARRATING A PAST STATE — `formerly`, `previously`,
#       `historically`, `until recently`, `used to be`, `before the fix`,
#       `cutover`. A public artefact describes what IS. See the section on R5
#       below for the marker set, for the one marker the sibling carries that
#       this repository may not, and for why.
#
# AND FOUR THINGS THAT ARE COUNTED RATHER THAN BANNED. Each is a construction
# that spells archaeology and live English with the same letters here, so each
# gets a census a human adjudicates instead of a rule that would either redden
# on correct prose or say nothing:
#
#   C1  `has since` / `have since` — PRINTED, NEVER RED. It is the marker BOTH
#       legitimate past-tense uses on this site reach for. See below.
#   C2  a bare `used to`, the copula excluded because R5 already owns it —
#       PRINTED, NEVER RED. The sibling's declared gap, carried here as the
#       sibling now carries it: as a number rather than as a sentence nobody
#       greps for.
#   C3  `no longer` — PRINTED, NEVER RED. A withdrawal already made and a live
#       fact about a live wire are spelled with the same two words, and the
#       live reading is the common one here. See below.
#   C4  a version number DATING A CLAIM — `since 0.4`, `until 0.4`, `from 1.0`.
#       PRINTED, NEVER RED. A version that SCOPES a rule a reader is bound by
#       is the sentence working; a version that STAMPS a fact which is simply
#       true is archaeology with the fact left live underneath it. See below.
#
# AND ONE EXEMPTION INSIDE EVERY RULE: a line carrying a live `TODO` or `FIXME`
# marker. The workspace rule that governs such a marker REQUIRES it to name the
# row that will drain it, so banning the citation there would set one rule
# against another. Zero such lines exist in this repository today; the carve-out
# is here so that obeying one rule cannot break the other.
#
# ── NOT PORTED, AND THE MEASUREMENT THAT SETTLED IT ──────────────────────────
#
# The sibling guard carries two further rules — a ban on naming a RETIRED WIRE
# VERSION, and a ban on a sentence NARRATING A PAST STATE. The first is not
# carried here and never can be; the second is carried, minus one marker, as R5.
#
# A RETIRED WIRE VERSION IS THIS REPOSITORY'S SUBJECT MATTER. It publishes every
# frozen cut of the skill, back through the 0.2 and 0.3 wires, and a cut names
# the wire it speaks. MEASURED over the tracked tree with the exemptions above
# applied: a retired-wire pattern matches in 23 files, FOURTEEN of them
# published cuts. A published cut is frozen — its bytes are pinned by digest
# from the sibling repository and can never be edited — so such a rule would
# produce a permanently red tree with no legal repair in it. That is the general
# constraint this file works under and it is worth stating on its own: A RULE
# THAT WOULD REDDEN ON A FROZEN CUT IS A WRONG RULE, because the cut cannot
# move. Anyone adding a rule here checks it against the cuts first.
#
# A PAST-STATE NARRATION BAN is a lexical proxy, so its marker set had to be
# re-argued against these pages rather than inherited. That argument is the next
# section, and it is where R5 and C1 come from.
#
# ── R5 AND C1-C4: A PUBLIC ARTEFACT DESCRIBES WHAT IS ────────────────────────
#
# The afterlife of a removed wire element, verb, endpoint or configuration key
# is ONE SHORT CHANGELOG LINE and nothing else — not a paragraph on a normative
# page, not a comment explaining what a declaration used to be. A reader
# arriving today has no memory to correct, and being told about a version of
# this protocol they never met, ahead of the one they did, is the page failing
# at its only job.
#
# WHY THIS IS A GUARD AND NOT A CONVENTION, in this repository specifically.
# The rule was already written down when the highest-value instance of the class
# yet found was found HERE: the narrative specification and Section 4.2 of the
# formal specification each opened the modules-never-verb-names rule by
# narrating the design that preceded it, and a third instance sat in a CSS
# comment the landing page serves in view-source. Those are the documents a
# third party is told to build against. Nothing read a sentence on them.
#
#   R5 — A CONSTRUCTION THAT ANNOUNCES A PAST STATE. A LEXICAL PROXY, and
#   written down as one: it matches a phrase, not a meaning. Every marker in it
#   was kept because it has no reading in which a live sentence on this site
#   needs it — a caveat a reader can still meet can always be stated in the
#   present, and stating it in the present is the whole point.
#
#   WHY `has since` IS NOT IN THE SET, AND WHAT TOOK ITS PLACE. The sibling
#   bans `has since been`. This repository may not, and the reason is a
#   measurement rather than a preference: `has since` is the marker BOTH
#   legitimate past-tense uses on this site reach for. MEASURED over the tracked
#   tree with the exemptions above applied, `has since` / `have since` is FIVE
#   lines, and FOUR of them are correct prose. Two are the PER-CUT HISTORY
#   CLAUSE — the narrative specification's version-lines paragraph and
#   `skills.md`'s cut table, each saying that earlier cuts describe wires that
#   have since changed. A frozen cut is a thing a reader can still FETCH, so a
#   past tense about one is the present tense of a permanent artefact. One is a
#   LIVE OPERATOR CAVEAT — the `verb_not_found` page telling a client that the
#   name it dialed may be a verb an OPERATOR has withdrawn, which is what that
#   page is FOR. One is a live caveat on the landing page, qualifying a recorded
#   live-assistant run as having been driven against a wire the demos have since
#   replaced, which is the honest reading of a claim a reader can still check.
#   The fifth is genuine narration.
#
#   A CARVE-OUT WAS CONSIDERED AND REFUSED. Nothing lexical separates «a verb
#   that has since been withdrawn», said of an OPERATOR's verb, from the same
#   clause said of ours; a carve-out that has to guess the subject is a rule
#   that fires wrong later, on a page nobody is looking at. Four correct lines
#   to one defect is not a ban, it is a census — so `has since` is COUNTED (C1)
#   and the one narration row it prints is a candidate finding a human reads.
#   This is the same shape as the frozen-cut refusal above, one layer down: a
#   rule that reddens where nothing can honestly be repaired is a wrong rule.
#
#   R5 IS INERT ON THE FROZEN CUTS, WHICH IS THE FIRST THING IT WAS CHECKED
#   AGAINST. MEASURED over `skill-v*.md` with `git grep -nIP`: the marker set
#   matches ZERO lines, and so does `has since been`, and so does a bare
#   `used to`. A cut describes a wire in the present tense of its own day — it
#   does not narrate the day before. A self-test arm re-asks this on every run
#   rather than trusting the sentence, and a hit on a cut is printed as the RULE
#   being wrong, because the bytes cannot move. C3 and C4 are the exception and
#   it is not a contradiction: a cut says what its own wire no longer carries
#   and scopes its own rules by version, in the present tense of its day. That
#   is why they are censuses and why those same bytes can hold their patterns.
#
#   C2 — A BARE `used to`. The sibling declines to ban this because it is also
#   the ordinary passive «employed in order to», and nothing lexical forks the
#   two. That holds here, so it is counted here — and the decision does NOT
#   rest on how many lines are in scope, which is why no number is typed into
#   this paragraph. A pattern that spells a violation and a correct sentence
#   with the SAME LETTERS cannot be a ban at ANY count: the exit code would
#   then turn on a distinction the pattern is unable to draw, and one line or a
#   hundred, the census is the honest instrument. The count is PRINTED by the
#   run — so it can drain, and so it can be SEEN to drain — and it is printed
#   rather than typed because this header's own rule is that an integer typed
#   into a comment is a measurement that stops being one the day after it is
#   typed. This paragraph once argued from such an integer, and the backlog it
#   described drained inside a day while the sentence stayed.
#
#   C3 — `no longer`. MEASURED when this census was added: FOURTEEN lines
#   outside the frozen cuts, of which TWO were narration and are gone. The
#   other twelve are correct and stay — a CDN serving verbs an operator no
#   longer offers (twice), a name a later revision can no longer claim (three
#   times, one of them inside a normative JSON Schema's own `description`), an
#   operator that no longer knows an unlinked key, five per-cut history clauses
#   that `check-skill-immutability` pass 4 additionally pins, and one line of
#   the working skill alias, which carries the newest cut's exact bytes and so
#   cannot move without a version bump. A ban would have reddened twelve true
#   sentences to catch two datings — and THIRTY frozen cuts besides, which
#   cannot move at all. That is the frozen-cut refusal above arriving from the
#   other side, and it is why this is a census and always will be. Do not read
#   those integers as current; run the guard, which prints them.
#
#   C4 — A VERSION NUMBER DATING A CLAIM, and it is the shape nothing here
#   reached before: not a past tense, not a withdrawal, just a live sentence
#   with a version stamped on the front of it. «Since 0.4 the input schema is
#   enforced» is TRUE today, and it dates that truth against a wire the reader
#   never used — the fact is live and only the stamp is archaeology, which is
#   exactly why R5's markers cannot see it. It cannot be a ban either, and for
#   the ordinary reason: the SAME construction scopes the rules a reader is
#   bound by. «Before 1.0 the additivity promise does not bind» and «from 1.0
#   onward the wire stays backward-compatible» are the version doing its job,
#   and they outnumber the datings on these pages. So: counted, adjudicated by
#   whether the version SCOPES a rule or STAMPS a fact.
#
#   WHY THE WORD BOUNDARY AND THE HYPHEN GUARD ARE PART OF THE PATTERNS AND NOT
#   DETAILS, both inherited from the sibling with the false rows that bought
#   them: unbounded, `used to` also matches the tail of «ref-used to» and
#   «ab-used to»; `previously-claimed` is a claim earlier in THIS run, not our
#   past; and «no longer-lived credential» is a comparative, not a withdrawal.
#   Each has its own self-test arm.
#
#   AND A CENSUS HAS NO EXIT CODE, SO SOMETHING ELSE HAS TO HOLD ITS PATTERN.
#   A zero prints the same whether the backlog drained or the pattern stopped
#   matching, and unlike a ban nothing goes red to say which. The probes below
#   catch a pattern that no longer matches the sentence written beside it; they
#   cannot catch one narrowed until it still matches that sentence and nothing
#   anybody writes. THE FROZEN CUTS ANSWER THAT, and only because another rule
#   froze them: they are in scope, they are real prose nobody wrote for this
#   purpose, and they CANNOT BE EDITED, so a count over them never falls
#   because somebody repaired something. Each census therefore declares which
#   side of that corpus it is on — C1 and C2 ABSENT, C3 and C4 PRESENT — and
#   every run re-measures both directions. See THE FROZEN CUTS AS A VACUITY
#   CORPUS, beside the code.
#
# ── THE DETECTOR ARMS, AND THE POLARITY THEY EXIST FOR ───────────────────────
#
# THIS IS A BAN, SO ITS COUNT IS PERMANENTLY ZERO WHEN IT WORKS. «No violations»
# and «this rule stopped matching anything» print the same clean zero, forever,
# and no census of the tree can separate them.
#
# THE SIBLING'S ANSWER DOES NOT WORK HERE, and that is a measurement, not an
# opinion. It re-runs each pattern over a corpus that is OUTSIDE the ban and
# cannot honestly drain — its journal and its tooling — and requires a match.
# The same corpus in this repository is the five exemptions above, and the run
# PRINTS what each pattern finds in it rather than quoting a number here: an
# integer typed into a comment is a measurement that stops being one the day
# after it is typed, and the four this paragraph used to carry had already gone
# stale in the commit that introduced them.
#
# WHAT THE TABLE SHOWS, AND IT IS WORSE FOR A CORPUS ARM THAN THE STALE
# INTEGERS WERE. The journal carries ledger ids, architecture-decision
# references and mentions of the maintainer in quantity, so three rules would
# be held by a corpus arm. The Cyrillic count is not zero — but every Cyrillic
# line in that corpus is in THIS FILE, in R3's own probe and its own self-test
# fixtures. A corpus arm for R3 would therefore be satisfied by the rule's own
# fixtures and by nothing else, which is not a weak arm but a CIRCULAR one:
# exactly the "a probe derived from the rule it tests moves whenever the rule
# does" failure this file warns about two paragraphs down. Run the guard to see
# the table; do not read it out of this comment.
#
# SO EACH RULE RE-PROVES ITS OWN DETECTOR, ON EVERY RUN, AGAINST FIXTURES THAT
# ARE NOT IN THE TREE AT ALL. Every rule declares two kinds of them beside its
# pattern: a PROBE, one spelling of the shape people actually write, which the
# rule MUST flag; and a FOIL, the nearest thing that is not a violation, which
# it must NOT. A pattern edited into something that no longer matches real prose
# fails its probe; one widened until it swallows a lookalike fails its foil.
# Neither answer depends on the tree, so neither can be manufactured by an
# obeyed ban — which is what the corpus arm could not promise.
#
# A RULE THAT IS AN ALTERNATION DECLARES A PROBE PER BRANCH, and R5 is why the
# fixtures are a LIST rather than a single sentence. One probe against a
# seven-way alternation proves one branch and reports a clean pass for the other
# six, so a marker quietly deleted from the set would be exactly as invisible as
# the ban it was supposed to enforce. Each of R5's markers therefore carries its
# own sentence, and each of its two boundary conditions its own foil — as do the
# four legitimate live sentences the marker set had to be argued against, which
# are written out here so that a later widening of the pattern reddens THIS file
# rather than the published page. The counted constructions carry the same
# fixtures for the same reason: a census whose pattern has stopped matching
# prints the same clean zero a drained backlog does.
#
# THE FIXTURES ARE INDEPENDENT OF THE PATTERNS ON PURPOSE. A probe derived from
# the rule it tests moves whenever the rule does and proves nothing; these are
# written out as literal sentences, so editing the pattern is what breaks them.
# That is also why the corpus census below is PRINTED and never red: it is worth
# reading, and it is not what holds anything.
#
# The remaining arms are about the guard looking at something real: the file set
# is non-empty, each exemption covers at least one tracked file, each published
# SURFACE CLASS is reached, and the frozen cuts are in scope.
#
# Usage:
#   bin/check-published-archaeology              # exit 0 clean, 1 on any violation
#   bin/check-published-archaeology --list       # print the scope, the exemptions and
#                                                # the counted constructions, row by row
#   bin/check-published-archaeology --self-test  # plant one violation per rule, in
#                                                # every place one can hide, and prove
#                                                # each is caught and each lookalike is not
#
# Stdlib only: no bundle, no services, no network.

require "open3"

REPO = File.expand_path("..", __dir__)

# ── the rules ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# One spelling of each shape, so there is a single thing to be wrong about.

LEDGER_ID_RE  = /\b[KT]-\d{3,4}\b/
ADR_RE        = /\bADR-\d{4}\b/
CYRILLIC_RE   = /[Ѐ-ӿ]/
MAINTAINER_RE = /\bPhil\b/

# A construction that announces a past state. A LEXICAL PROXY — see the header
# for the marker set's argument against these pages, for the one marker the
# sibling carries that this repository may not (`has since`, counted as C1
# below), and for the two boundary conditions written into the pattern.
PAST_NARRATION_RE =
  /\b(?:formerly|previously(?!-)|historically|until\s+recently|(?<!-)used\s+to\s+be|
       before\s+the\s+fix|cutover)\b/ix

# COUNTED, NEVER BANNED. `has since` is the marker BOTH legitimate past-tense
# uses on this site reach for; a bare `used to` is also the ordinary passive
# «employed in order to». The copula is excluded here because R5 owns it and one
# sentence must not be reported twice.
HAS_SINCE_RE     = /\b(?:ha[sv]e?|had)\s+since\b/i
BARE_USED_TO_RE  = /\b(?<!-)used\s+to\b(?!\s+be\b)/i

# `no longer` states a live fact about a live wire far more often than it
# narrates, and every frozen cut carries it, so it can never be a ban. The
# hyphen guard is the pattern and not a detail: «no longer-lived credential» is
# a comparative, not a withdrawal.
NO_LONGER_RE = /\bno\s+longer\b(?!-)/i

# A VERSION NUMBER DATING A CLAIM. Both readings are spelled alike: «before 1.0
# this promise does not bind» SCOPES a rule that binds a reader today, and
# «since 0.4 the input schema is enforced» DATES a fact that is simply true,
# against a version the reader never used. Only the second is archaeology, and
# nothing lexical forks them. Declared limit: this cannot tell a version number
# from any other decimal, so «after 3.0 hours» is counted too — one glance for
# a human, which is the price of a census that never reddens.
VERSION_DATING_RE =
  /\b(?:since|as\s+of|starting\s+(?:in|with|from)|beginning\s+(?:in|with)|
       through|until|till|prior\s+to|up\s+to|from|before|after)\s+
     (?:protocol\s+|version\s+|v)?\d+\.\d+(?:\.\d+)?\b/ix

# A page's credit slot: the name as the TEXT of a link. See the header for why
# this is a shape rather than a path, and for the limit it declares.
BYLINE_RE = %r{<a\b[^>]*\bhref\s*=[^>]*>[^<]*\bPhil\b[^<]*</a>}i

# A live TODO/FIXME is REQUIRED to carry the id of the row that drains it.
TODO_RE = /\b(?:TODO|FIXME)\b/

RULES = [
  {
    id: "R1",
    re: LEDGER_ID_RE,
    what: "a ledger id",
    none: "no ledger id",
    remedy: "the ledger it names is private and a reader cannot open it. Drop the citation and " \
            "keep the sentence that states the rule",
    probe: "The past-slot filter refuses a booking whose start has gone by (K-480), and T-1027 " \
           "widened it.",
    foil: "OK-1234 is a fixture id, RFC 8628 is a standard, CVE-2021-0001 is neither, and K-12 " \
          "has too few digits to be a row.",
  },
  {
    id: "R2",
    re: ADR_RE,
    what: "a reference to a numbered architecture decision",
    none: "no architecture-decision reference",
    remedy: "the decision records live in a private repository. State the rule the decision " \
            "settled, in the present tense",
    probe: "The refusal shape is the one fixed by ADR-0018 and restated here.",
    foil: "ADR-12 is too short and ADR-99999 too long to be one of those references.",
  },
  {
    id: "R3",
    re: CYRILLIC_RE,
    what: "Cyrillic prose",
    none: "no Cyrillic",
    remedy: "this site publishes in English. Say the same thing in English, as a statement about " \
            "the protocol rather than as a quotation",
    probe: "The ruling, verbatim: «убери из демок всю археологию».",
    foil: "The window is said out loud — «08:00-10:00 (Europe/Dublin)» — and a naive reader gets " \
          "the same bytes. Arrows and currency travel too: <- -> OK, EUR 200.",
  },
  {
    id: "R4",
    re: MAINTAINER_RE,
    exempt: BYLINE_RE,
    what: "the maintainer named in prose",
    none: "no maintainer named in prose",
    remedy: "naming a person asks a stranger to trust a judgement instead of reading a rule. " \
            "State what the rule IS and what follows from it",
    probe: "Phil accepted this control's weakness openly, and the page says so.",
    foil: %(    Built by <a href="https://example.invalid">Phil Pirozhkov</a>.),
  },
  {
    id: "R5",
    re: PAST_NARRATION_RE,
    what: "a sentence narrating a past state",
    none: "no narration of a past state",
    remedy: "a reader arriving today has no memory to correct. State the rule and its reason in " \
            "the present; the removed thing gets one changelog line and nothing else",
    # ONE PROBE PER MARKER. A single sentence against a seven-way alternation
    # proves one branch and reports a clean pass for the other six.
    probe: [
      "The catalog was formerly behind a Bearer token.",
      "A per-verb endpoint previously resolved identity before it resolved a name.",
      "Historically the discovery document withheld the registered verb names.",
      "Until recently the envelope carried a kind discriminator.",
      "It used to be the other way round: the premise is retired.",
      "Before the fix an unregistered name answered with a problem document.",
      "The per-verb cutover moved the prose everywhere and left one example behind.",
    ],
    # THE FOUR LIVE SENTENCES THE MARKER SET WAS ARGUED AGAINST, plus the two
    # boundary conditions the pattern writes down. Widening R5 to swallow any of
    # these reddens THIS file rather than a published page.
    foil: [
      "0.4.10 on this same wire but describing a descriptor slot that has since been withdrawn.",
      "0.4.0-0.4.2 describe wires that have since changed, and the same-wire chain runs on.",
      "A typo, a name from a different operator, or a verb that has since been withdrawn.",
      "Against the 0.3 wire these demos have since replaced; no 0.4 run is recorded yet.",
      "Release a previously-claimed id, because that claim was made earlier in this same run.",
      "The RSA key cross-used to be minted twice is refused, and so is the key used to sign it.",
    ],
  },
].freeze

# ── counted, never banned ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Same shape as a rule, so `scan_text` and the detector arms take them
# unchanged; they simply never reach the exit code. A census whose pattern has
# stopped matching prints the same clean zero a drained backlog does, so each
# carries fixtures for exactly the reason the rules do.

COUNTED = [
  {
    id: "C1",
    re: HAS_SINCE_RE,
